      "id": 1,
      "text": "During the Revolutionary War, Continental troops built a fort on Marble Hill that the British seized and renamed Fort Prince Charles.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marble_Hill%2C_Manhattan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The New York Central and Hudson River Railroad opened Marble Hill station in 1905–1906, replacing the older Kingsbridge stop on the Spuyten Duyvil line.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marble_Hill%2C_Manhattan",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Marble Hill’s small footprint belies a history of glacial rock, quarrying, and the 19th‑century canal that reshaped Manhattan’s edge.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityneighborhoods.nyc/marble-hill",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Quarries along Spuyten Duyvil Creek mined marble that later appeared in landmarks such as the Statue of Liberty’s pedestal.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.cityneighborhoods.nyc/marble-hill",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Because no firehouse existed, residents formed the Marble Hill Hose Company in 1895 to protect their neighborhood.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marble_Hill%2C_Manhattan",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The first major residential project, Marble Hill Houses, opened in 1952, providing thousands of homes and shaping the area’s skyline.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
